const story=(id,title,skill,editions,reflect,related)=>({id,title,question:'Can I read '+title+'?',category:'stories',kind:'story',minutes:3,tag:'Original story · '+skill,words:'story stories fiction kenya '+title.toLowerCase()+' '+skill.toLowerCase(),fact:skill,source:'ZUNO original fiction · fictional characters',url:null,levels:Object.fromEntries(Object.entries(editions).map(([k,v])=>[k,v[0]])),sectionsByAge:Object.fromEntries(Object.entries(editions).map(([k,v])=>[k,v.slice(1)])),sections:editions['9-11'].slice(1),reflect,related});
export const stories=[
story('amani-rain','Amani and the Missing Rain','Observe before you decide',{
 '6-8':['The garden was wet, but Amani’s rain cup was empty. “The rain missed my cup!” he said. His friend Wanjiku looked up. A wide leaf hung over it like a tiny roof.', ['A better place','With their teacher, they moved the cup into an open space. The next rainy day, it collected water. “The cup was fine,” Amani said. “Its place was the problem.”'],['A small discovery','They drew the leaf and their two cup positions. Amani crossed out “broken cup” in his notebook. He wrote, “Look carefully first.” Wanjiku added a picture of a very helpful leaf.']],
 '9-11':['Amani was sure his rain gauge was broken. The school garden in Nakuru was soaked, yet the measuring cup held barely a drop. “We need a new one,” he announced. Wanjiku asked a different question: “Where did the water go?”', ['The clue above their heads','They checked for a hole. None. They checked the markings. Fine. Then a breeze lifted a broad leaf above the cup. Water dripped from its edge onto the soil, missing the cup completely. The leaf had been an excellent umbrella.'],['A fairer test','Their teacher helped them choose an open spot. They recorded the position and checked again after the next rainfall. This time the cup collected water. They had changed the location, so they still needed more observations before making big claims. Amani kept the old cup. He changed the first line of his report instead: “Our measurement did not represent the rain reaching the whole garden.”']],
 '12-14':['The sensor report said “almost no rain”. The muddy school garden suggested otherwise. Amani wanted to replace the homemade gauge. Wanjiku wanted to inspect the measurement before blaming the equipment. Their disagreement became the beginning of a useful investigation.', ['A reading is not the whole world','There was no visible leak. The scale was legible. The gauge stood under an overhanging leaf that diverted water away from its opening. The device could be working while the placement made its reading misleading. They photographed only the equipment, with their teacher’s permission, and noted the location.'],['A more careful conclusion','They moved the gauge to an appropriate open position and planned repeated observations. One later rainfall could not prove that every difference was caused by the leaf: the rainfall itself might differ. For a stronger comparison, they would need similar gauges exposed at the same time and a consistent method. Their final report did not say “technology failed”. It said, “The measurement setup limited what we could conclude.” That sounded less dramatic and much more useful.']]},
 {'6-8':'What was acting like a roof? What would you look at before calling the cup broken?','9-11':'Why was inspecting the location useful? What else would you record next time?','12-14':'How would simultaneous measurements improve the test? What uncertainty would remain?'},['water-cycle','sensors','data']),
story('nia-robot','Nia’s Robot Takes the Long Way','Debug one step at a time',{
 '6-8':['Nia’s little rover was meant to deliver a paper star. Instead, it drove into a cushion. “You silly robot!” she laughed. Then she looked at her arrows.', ['The wrong turn','Her third arrow pointed left. It should have pointed right. Nia changed just that arrow and tried again. The rover rolled around the cushion.'],['A new kind of win','This time it reached the star. Nia smiled, but her favourite part was finding the mistake. The robot had followed the instructions she gave it. Now she knew how to make them better.']],
 '9-11':['At the school maker club, Nia built a cardboard rover to carry a paper star across a floor grid. Her friend Musa counted down. The rover moved forward, turned left and bumped gently into a cushion. “It ignored me,” Nia said. Musa pointed at the program. “Did it?”', ['Follow the arrows','They traced each instruction with a finger. At step three, the program said left. The route on Nia’s drawing said right. Instead of rewriting everything, she changed one instruction. The next run reached the final square, but stopped one square before the star.'],['Two bugs, two discoveries','Nia added the missing move and ran the program again. Success. She wrote down both bugs and the fixes so another learner could follow the process. “A mistake is information,” she told Musa. “Provided we stop and listen to it.” They celebrated with a very slow robot victory dance.']],
 '12-14':['Nia’s delivery rover worked once, then failed during the maker-club demonstration. Her first instinct was to change several instructions and increase the speed. Musa suggested something less exciting: reproduce the failure and record what happened.', ['Predict, run, compare','They wrote the expected position after each command and compared it with the observed route. A reversed turn explained the first collision. After correcting it, they found an independent off-by-one error at the destination. Fixing several things at once would have made the cause harder to identify.'],['Success needs more than one example','The rover reached the star, but Nia was not finished. She tested another starting position and an obstructed path. Their simple program assumed a clear route and a fixed start. She documented those limits instead of calling it “ready for any delivery”. The demonstration ended with a better lesson than a flawless run: reliable systems grow through clear assumptions, useful tests and careful changes.']]},
 {'6-8':'Which arrow would you check first if the rover turned the wrong way?','9-11':'Why did Nia change one instruction at a time?','12-14':'Which test would reveal an assumption that the successful demonstration missed?'},['algorithms','data','ai']),
story('message-mystery','The Message That Grew Legs','Check before you share',{
 '6-8':['“The library is closing forever!” said a message on a tablet. Sifa felt sad. Before sending it to her friends, she showed it to her aunt.', ['A missing little word','Together they checked the library’s notice. It said, “Closed on Monday for repairs.” The library would open again. The message had left out an important part.'],['A useful pause','Sifa did not pass the scary message along. She drew a tiny pause button in her notebook: stop, check, then decide. Some messages move very fast. A careful thinker does not have to.']],
 '9-11':['The message arrived in capitals: OUR LIBRARY IS CLOSING. Everyone in Sifa’s reading club started guessing why. Someone added “forever”. Someone else said the books were already gone. Sifa realised that nobody had checked the original notice.', ['Go back to the source','With her aunt, she found the library’s announcement. It was closing for one day while repairs were carried out. The date on the circulating screenshot had also been cropped away. A small true detail had grown into a very different story.'],['Correct it kindly','Sifa explained what she found without mocking the person who shared the message. “I thought it meant forever too,” she said. “Here’s the complete notice.” The reading club chose three questions for next time: Who said it? When? What is missing? The next meeting happened on Tuesday, exactly as planned.']],
 '12-14':['By lunchtime, a cropped screenshot had convinced Sifa’s reading club that their library would close permanently. The claim was emotionally convincing, repeatedly shared and unsupported by an identifiable source. Sifa noticed that repetition was being treated as evidence.', ['Context changes a claim','With an adult, she checked the library’s official announcement and its date. The closure was temporary maintenance. The crop removed both the time period and the reason. Sifa separated the accurate fragment, “the building will close”, from the unsupported conclusion, “the library is ending”.'],['Update without humiliating','She shared the complete context with the club, avoiding screenshots that exposed people’s private contact details. She also corrected her own earlier guess. Their new habit was to distinguish source, evidence and interpretation before forwarding a claim. It would not make them immune to misinformation. It gave them a method for noticing uncertainty and changing their minds without turning a mistake into a public punishment.']]},
 {'6-8':'Who could help you check a surprising message?','9-11':'How did the missing date change the story?','12-14':'What is the difference between a true fragment and a supported conclusion?'},['online-safety','data','ai']),
story('lunchbox-library','The Library in a Lunchbox','Design for the people using it',{
 '6-8':['At reading club, the internet stopped. “No stories today?” asked Baraka. His teacher opened a small box of books and a tablet with saved stories.', ['Stories can stay','The tablet did not need the internet to open stories already saved on it. Baraka chose one. His friend preferred a paper book. Both began reading.'],['Room for everyone','Baraka drew his dream library in a lunchbox. It had books, large words, pictures and a place for headphones. “The cleverest library,” he said, “is one everyone can use.”']],
 '9-11':['Baraka wanted to build the world’s cleverest reading club. His plan had a website, moving stars and a giant video button. Then the internet stopped during the very first meeting. His friend Leila asked, “Can we still read anything?”', ['A different design question','Their teacher showed them approved stories saved on a tablet for offline use. They also had paper copies. Baraka realised that his beautiful loading screen was not helping anyone read. He began a new list: work without a connection, make the words readable and let readers choose their pace.'],['A useful little library','Their next model fitted inside a decorated lunchbox. It held paper booklets and a card showing how the digital collection would work. Leila tested it with a younger reader, who found a confusing button immediately. Baraka changed the button. His project had fewer moving stars now and many more actual readers.']],
 '12-14':['Baraka’s prototype looked impressive on a fast connection. At the reading club, its opening video stalled and its catalogue never appeared. He had optimised the demonstration instead of the experience people actually needed.', ['Start with the constraint','The group listed real conditions: shared devices, intermittent connectivity, different reading abilities and limited time. With their teacher, they explored permission-cleared offline content and paper alternatives. Offline access solved one problem, but introduced others: storage limits, updates and checking that old content did not stay indefinitely.'],['Design is a conversation','They tested a small prototype before building a larger system. A younger reader misunderstood an icon. Another reader needed larger type. Someone wanted to stop narration without losing their place. Each observation changed the design. Baraka kept a lunchbox-sized model on his desk as a reminder: useful technology begins with people’s circumstances, and a beautiful interface still has to work.']]},
 {'6-8':'What would you put in a library that everyone could enjoy?','9-11':'What did the younger reader teach the designers?','12-14':'Which trade-offs come with offline content? How would you test your assumptions?'},['internet','online-safety','algorithms']),
story('moving-star','Sifa and the Wandering Light','Separate observation from explanation',{
 '6-8':['At dusk, Sifa noticed a bright point in the sky. “A star is following us!” she said. Her grandfather smiled. “Let’s look, and write down what we notice.”', ['A sky notebook','They watched from a safe spot after sunset. Sifa drew the light beside a tree. On another evening, they looked again. They did not guess its name from brightness alone.'],['Another question','With help, they checked a trusted sky guide for their place and date. Some bright points are planets, not stars. Sifa’s notebook ended with a new question: “What else have I been calling by the wrong name?”']],
 '9-11':['Sifa called every bright dot a star until one evening when her grandfather asked, “What evidence would tell you?” She suggested brightness. He reminded her that a nearby torch could look brighter than a distant streetlight. Brightness alone would not settle it.', ['Record before naming','From a safe viewing place after sunset, they noted the date, time and the light’s position beside a familiar landmark. They compared observations on another evening. Then an adult helped them consult a trustworthy sky guide for their location and date.'],['A careful answer','The guide showed that a bright planet could be visible in that part of the sky. Sifa learned that planets shine mainly by reflecting sunlight. She also learned to distinguish what she saw from what she thought it meant. Her drawing said “bright point”. Her explanation said “possibly a planet; check the guide”. The little word “possibly” made her notebook stronger.']],
 '12-14':['A bright point above the evening skyline started an argument in Sifa’s family: star, planet, aircraft or satellite? Every explanation sounded certain. Sifa proposed recording observations before choosing a label.', ['What would distinguish the possibilities?','They noted time, direction, apparent movement and conditions from a safe location. An aircraft might show a changing position over a short interval; a planet’s movement against distant stars requires different observations. Brightness and twinkling can offer clues, but atmospheric conditions and viewing circumstances complicate them.'],['Confidence should follow evidence','An adult helped compare their notes with a reliable sky guide for the actual place and date. Sifa did not turn a useful clue into a universal rule. Her conclusion included the evidence, the likely explanation and what she had not checked. Science had not removed the wonder from the sky. It had given her a better way to follow it.']]},
 {'6-8':'Can two things look alike but be different?','9-11':'Which parts of Sifa’s notes were observations, and which were guesses?','12-14':'What additional observation would help distinguish a planet from a moving aircraft?'},['solar-system','sun','data']),
story('chapati-challenge','The Great Chapati Challenge','Explain your thinking',{
 '6-8':['Malaika drew a round chapati on paper. “Two pieces for two friends!” she said. She drew a line very close to one edge. One piece was tiny. The other was huge.', ['Two is not always equal','“We both have one piece,” said her brother. “But are the pieces the same size?” Malaika looked again. She drew another chapati and divided it through the middle.'],['A fairer picture','Now the two pieces were equal halves. They tried four equal quarters next. The best part was not getting the answer first. It was explaining why the new picture was fairer.']],
 '9-11':['Malaika’s maths club had one rule: explain your thinking. She drew a paper chapati and split it into two unequal pieces. “One piece each,” she announced. Her brother objected. Malaika pointed to the equal number of pieces. He pointed to their unequal size.', ['Count the right thing','They drew a second circle, divided into two equal parts. Each was one half. Then they drew four equal quarters and shaded two. The shaded amount matched one half, even though the number of pieces differed. Counting pieces alone had hidden the important relationship.'],['An answer you can defend','For their final challenge, they compared three quarters with two quarters of the same-sized whole. Malaika used a drawing instead of “three is bigger than two” as her entire explanation. The club agreed: a good answer should make sense to someone who did not already know it. Their real chapatis stayed in the kitchen. The paper ones did all the mathematical work.']],
 '12-14':['Malaika’s sharing puzzle began with a misleading statement: “Everyone gets one piece, so the shares are equal.” Her drawing made the problem obvious. Equal counts do not imply equal quantities when the pieces have different sizes.', ['Keep the whole in view','The club compared equivalent fractions using equal-sized paper wholes. Two quarters matched one half. But one half of a large whole need not equal one half of a smaller one. The denominator alone was not enough; the reference quantity mattered.'],['Make the assumption visible','They rewrote the puzzle to specify the size of the whole and the equality of the partitions. Then they asked whether a mathematically equal share always answers every question about fairness. Different needs can make a real-life sharing decision more complicated. Malaika’s final presentation separated the calculation from the value judgement. That was a better argument than treating a number as if it could decide the whole situation by itself.']]},
 {'6-8':'Do two pieces always mean two equal halves?','9-11':'Why must the drawings use the same-sized whole when comparing amounts?','12-14':'Which assumptions does an equal-share calculation need? Where does the fairness discussion go beyond arithmetic?'},['fractions','patterns','data'])
];
